            <ArticleTitle>Primary Colorectal Cells Culture as a Translational Research Model.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>97-105</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/978-1-4939-7477-1_8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Preclinical studies are an essential stage for any pharmacological agent hoping to make its way into clinical trials. An ideal preclinical model that can accurately predict clinical response does not exist and the best that the scientific community have at the moment is to select the most relevant study model pertaining to the disease of interest from those available, which includes: cell lines, animal models, and even in-silico methodology. Currently, there is a huge gap between preclinical and clinical trial results, indicating that there is much room for improvement in developing a better model to bridge the translational gap.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
